hadiths[63]{hadithnumber,text}:
"11714","Aishah, may Allah be pleased with her, reported that the Prophet, peace be upon him, said that nothing would remain after him of prophethood except al-mubashshirat. The Companions asked what al-mubashshirat were, and he explained that it is the righteous dream which the servant sees or which is shown to him. > **""Nothing remains after me of prophethood except al-mubashshirat. They are the righteous dream which the servant sees or which is shown to him.""**"
"11715","Abdullah ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them both, reported that the Prophet, peace be upon him, said that the righteous dream is one part of seventy parts of prophethood. > **""The righteous dream is one part of seventy parts of prophethood.""**"
"11716","Abdullah ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them both, reported that the Messenger of Allah, peace be upon him, said that the righteous dream is one part of forty-six parts of prophethood. > **""The righteous dream is one part of forty-six parts of prophethood.""**"
"11717","Sulayman ibn Arib reported from Abu Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him, that the Messenger of Allah, peace be upon him, said that the dream of the believing man is glad tidings from Allah and is one part of forty-six parts of prophethood. The narrator then mentioned this to Ibn Abbas, who replied that Abbas ibn Abd al-Muttalib reported from the Messenger of Allah, peace be upon him, that it is one part of fifty parts of prophethood. > **""The dream of the believing man is glad tidings from Allah; it is one part of forty-six parts of prophethood.""**"
"11718","Abu Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him, reported that the Messenger of Allah, peace be upon him, said that whoever sees him in sleep has truly seen him, for Satan cannot assume his form. He added that the truthful righteous dream of the believing servant is one part of seventy parts of prophethood. > **""Whoever sees me in sleep has truly seen me, for Satan cannot assume my form. The truthful righteous dream of the believing servant is one part of seventy parts of prophethood.""**"
"11719","Jabir, may Allah be pleased with him, reported that he heard the Messenger of Allah, peace be upon him, say that the dream of the believing man is a part of prophethood. > **""The dream of the believing man is a part of prophethood.""**"
"11720","Abu al-Tufayl, may Allah be pleased with him, reported that the Messenger of Allah, peace be upon him, said there is no prophethood after him except al-mubashshirat. When asked what they were, he said they are the good dream, or he said the righteous dream. > **""There is no prophethood after me except al-mubashshirat, and they are the righteous dream.""**"
"11721","Hudhayfah ibn Asid, may Allah be pleased with him, reported that the Messenger of Allah, peace be upon him, said that prophethood has gone and there is no prophethood after him except al-mubashshirat. When asked what they were, he said they are the righteous dream which a man sees or which is shown to him. > **""Prophethood has gone, and there is no prophethood after me except al-mubashshirat: the righteous dream which a man sees or which is shown to him.""**"
"11722","Samurah, may Allah be pleased with him, reported that the Messenger of Allah, peace be upon him, used to say to them that Abu Bakr interprets dreams, and that the righteous dream is a share of prophethood. > **""Abu Bakr interprets dreams, and the righteous dream is a share of prophethood.""**"
"11723","Abdullah ibn Masud, may Allah be pleased with him, reported that the Messenger of Allah, peace be upon him, said that the truthful or righteous dream is one part of seventy-six parts of prophethood, and in another wording one part of seventy parts. > **""The truthful righteous dream is one part of seventy-six parts of prophethood.""**"
"11724","Abdullah ibn Masud, may Allah be pleased with him, said that the righteous dream is one part of seventy parts of prophethood, that the scorching wind from which the jinn were created is one part of seventy parts of the Fire of Hell, and that this fire of yours is one part of seventy parts of the Fire of Hell. > **""The righteous dream is one part of seventy parts of prophethood.""**"
"11725","Anas ibn Malik, may Allah be pleased with him, reported that the Messenger of Allah, peace be upon him, said that the dream of the believer is one single part of seventy parts of prophethood. > **""The dream of the believer is one single part of seventy parts of prophethood.""**"
"11726","Abu Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him, reported from the Prophet, peace be upon him, that the dream of the believing servant is one part of forty parts of prophethood. > **""The dream of the believing servant is one part of forty parts of prophethood.""**"
"11727","Awf ibn Malik, may Allah be pleased with him, reported that the Messenger of Allah, peace be upon him, said that the dream of the believer is one part of forty-six parts of prophethood. > **""The dream of the believer is one part of forty-six parts of prophethood.""**"
"11728","Ubadah ibn al-Samit, may Allah be pleased with him, reported that the Messenger of Allah, peace be upon him, said that the dream of the believer is speech by which the servant's Lord speaks to him in sleep. > **""The dream of the believer is speech by which the servant's Lord speaks to him in sleep.""**"
"11729","Ibn Umar, may Allah be pleased with them both, reported that the Messenger of Allah, peace be upon him, said that among the gravest lies are for a man to claim descent from other than his father, to show his eyes what they did not see, and to alter the landmarks of the earth. > **""Among the gravest lies are claiming another father, showing the eyes what they did not see, and altering the landmarks of the earth.""**"
"11730","Ali, may Allah be pleased with him, reported from the Prophet, peace be upon him, that whoever deliberately lies about a dream will be burdened on the Day of Resurrection with tying a knot in a grain of barley. > **""Whoever deliberately lies about a dream will be burdened on the Day of Resurrection with tying a knot in a grain of barley.""**"
"11731","Abu Shurayh al-Khuzai, may Allah be pleased with him, reported that the Messenger of Allah, peace be upon him, said that among the most insolent people toward Allah are one who kills other than his killer, one who seeks pre-Islamic blood-revenge in Islam, and one who shows his eyes in sleep what they did not see. > **""Among the most insolent people toward Allah are one who kills other than his killer, one who seeks pre-Islamic blood-revenge in Islam, and one who shows his eyes in sleep what they did not see.""**"
"11732","Abdullah ibn Umar, may Allah be pleased with them both, reported that the Prophet, peace be upon him, said that the righteous dream is one part of seventy parts of prophethood. Whoever sees good should praise Allah and mention it, and whoever sees otherwise should seek refuge in Allah from the evil of his dream and not mention it, for then it will not harm him. > **""The righteous dream is one part of seventy parts of prophethood. Whoever sees good should praise Allah and mention it, and whoever sees otherwise should seek refuge in Allah from its evil and not mention it, for it will not harm him.""**"
"11733","Abdullah ibn Amr, may Allah be pleased with them both, reported from the Messenger of Allah, peace be upon him, concerning His saying about glad tidings in worldly life, that it is the righteous dream by which the believer is given glad tidings, one part of forty-nine parts of prophethood. Whoever sees such a dream should tell of it, and whoever sees otherwise, it is from Satan to grieve him, so he should spit to his left three times, remain silent, and not tell of it. > **""The righteous dream by which the believer is given glad tidings is one part of forty-nine parts of prophethood. Whoever sees it should tell of it, and whoever sees otherwise should spit to his left three times, remain silent, and not tell of it.""**"
"11734","Umm Salamah, may Allah be pleased with her, reported that the Messenger of Allah, peace be upon him, said that if one of you sees in his sleep what he dislikes, he should spit to his left three times and seek refuge from what he saw. > **""If one of you sees in his sleep what he dislikes, let him spit to his left three times and seek refuge from what he saw.""**"
"11735","Anas ibn Malik, may Allah be pleased with him, reported that a man came to the Messenger of Allah, peace be upon him, and said he saw dreams that made him ill. The Messenger of Allah, peace be upon him, replied that the good dream is from Allah and the bad dream is from Satan, so when one of you sees such a thing he should spit to his left three times and seek refuge in Allah from its evil, for then it will not harm him. > **""The good dream is from Allah and the bad dream is from Satan. If one of you sees such a thing, let him spit to his left three times and seek refuge in Allah from its evil, for it will not harm him.""**"
"11736","Anas, may Allah be pleased with him, reported that the Messenger of Allah, peace be upon him, loved good dreams and would often ask whether anyone among them had seen a dream. A woman came and said she had seen herself entering Paradise, hearing a commotion that shook Paradise, and then seeing twelve named men who had been sent out on an expedition being brought in worn garments with their veins bleeding. They were ordered to the land or river of al-Baydakh, dipped in it, and emerged with faces like the moon on the night of fullness. They sat on chairs of gold, were brought a tray of unripe dates, ate whatever fruit they wished, and she ate with them. Afterward the bearer of news from that expedition came and named the same twelve men as fallen. The Messenger of Allah, peace be upon him, then called for the woman and asked her to narrate her dream to the man, and it matched exactly what she had told him. > **""Bring the woman to me. Narrate your dream to him.""**"
"11737","Ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them both, said that the dreams of the Prophets are revelation. > **""The dreams of the Prophets are revelation.""**"
"11738","Abd al-Rahman ibn Aish reported from one of the Companions of the Prophet, peace be upon him, that the Messenger of Allah, peace be upon him, came out to them one morning cheerful and bright-faced. They remarked on his radiant face, and he said nothing prevented him, for his Lord had come to him in the night in the best form and said O Muhammad. He replied with presence and readiness. His Lord asked concerning what the highest assembly disputes, twice or thrice, then placed His hands between his shoulders until he felt their coolness in his chest, so that all in the heavens and earth became clear to him. He then recited the verse shown to Ibrahim of the kingdom of the heavens and earth. His Lord asked again, and he answered: concerning expiations. Asked what they are, he said: walking on foot to the congregations, sitting in the mosque beyond the prayers, and completing ablution thoroughly despite hardship. Whoever does so lives well, dies well, and becomes free of sin like the day his mother bore him. Among ranks are kind speech, spreading peace, feeding food, and praying at night while people sleep. He was then told that when he prays he should ask for good things, abandoning of evils, love of the poor, repentance, and to be taken untested if trial is decreed for the people. > **""O Allah, I ask You for good things, abandoning of evils, love of the poor, and that You turn to me in forgiveness. If You decree trial for the people, take me to You untested.""**"
"11739","Abd al-Rahman ibn Aish, may Allah be pleased with him, reported that the Messenger of Allah, peace be upon him, said he saw his Lord in the best form, Who said O Muhammad and asked concerning what the highest assembly disputes. He answered that his Lord knows best. A hand was placed between his shoulders until he felt its coolness in his chest, and he knew what is in the heavens and earth. He then recited the verse shown to Ibrahim. Asked again, he answered: concerning expiations, namely walking to the Friday gatherings, sitting in mosques beyond prayers, and completing ablution in hardship. Allah said whoever does so lives well, dies well, and becomes as sinless as the day his mother bore him. Among ranks are feeding food, spreading peace, and standing at night while people sleep. He was then taught to ask for righteous deeds, abandoning evils, love of the poor, forgiveness, mercy, repentance, and to be taken untested if trial is decreed. The Prophet, peace be upon him, added that they should learn these words, for by Him in Whose Hand is his soul they are truth. > **""O Allah, I ask You for righteous deeds, abandoning of evils, love of the poor, and that You forgive me, have mercy on me, and turn to me. If You decree trial for a people, take me to You untested. Learn these words, for by Him in Whose Hand is my soul, they are truth.""**"
"11740","In another narration, the Messenger of Allah, peace be upon him, came out to his Companions in the morning rejoicing, with joy recognized on his face, and mentioned the like of the previous account. In it he was told: when you pray, O Muhammad, say this supplication. In it the ranks were described as fasting and kind speech. > **""When you pray, O Muhammad, say this supplication.""**"
"11741","In another narration, Khalid ibn al-Lajlaj said he heard Abd al-Rahman ibn Aish say that the Messenger of Allah, peace be upon him, came out to them one morning, and he then mentioned the like of the preceding narration. > **""The Messenger of Allah came out to us one morning.""**"
"11742","Thawban, may Allah be pleased with him, reported that the Messenger of Allah, peace be upon him, came out to them after the morning prayer and said his Lord had come to him in the night in the best form and asked O Muhammad whether he knew concerning what the highest assembly disputes. He said no, then something was mentioned until all between heaven and earth was shown to him. He answered yes: they dispute concerning expiations and ranks. As for ranks, they are feeding food, spreading peace, and standing at night while people sleep. As for expiations, they are walking to the congregations, completing ablution despite aversion, and sitting in mosques after prayers. He was then told: O Muhammad, speak and you will be heard, ask and you will be given. He asked to be taught, and was taught to ask for righteous deeds, abandoning evils, love of the poor, forgiveness, mercy, to be taken untested if trial is decreed, and for the love of Allah, the love of whoever loves Him, and love that conveys His love. > **""O Muhammad, speak and you will be heard, ask and you will be given. O Allah, I ask You for righteous deeds, abandoning of evils, love of the poor, and that You forgive me and have mercy on me. If You decree trial for a people, take me to You untested. O Allah, I ask You for Your love, the love of whoever loves You, and love that conveys Your love to me.""**"
"11743","Ibn Umar, may Allah be pleased with them both, reported that the Messenger of Allah, peace be upon him, was delayed from his Companions for the dawn prayer until they said the sun had risen. He then came out, led them in dawn prayer, told them to remain in their rows, and asked whether they knew what had detained him. They said Allah and His Messenger know best. He said he had prayed in his place of prayer, drowsiness overcame him, and his Lord came in the best form, said O Muhammad, and he answered with presence. Asked concerning what the highest assembly disputes, he said he did not know. A hand was placed between his shoulders until he felt its coolness in his chest, and he knew what he had been asked. Asked again, he answered: concerning expiations and ranks. Expiations are completing ablution despite aversion, walking to congregations, and sitting in mosques after prayers. Ranks are feeding food, kind speech, and prostration at night while people sleep. His Lord said ask Me, O Muhammad, and he asked for righteous deeds, abandoning evils, love of the poor, forgiveness, mercy, to be taken untested if trial is decreed, for His love and the love of deeds drawing near His love, for faith that touches the heart so he knows only what is decreed befalls him, and contentment with His decree. > **""Remain in your rows. Do you know what detained me from you? Ask Me, O Muhammad. O Allah, I ask You for righteous deeds, abandoning of evils, love of the poor, and that You forgive me and have mercy on me. If You decree trial for a people, take me untested. O Allah, I ask You for Your love, the love of deeds drawing me near Your love, faith that touches my heart, and contentment with Your decree.""**"
"11744","Abu Umamah, may Allah be pleased with him, reported from the Prophet, peace be upon him, that his Lord came to him in the best form and said O Muhammad. He answered with presence. Asked concerning what the highest assembly disputes, he said he did not know. A hand was placed upon his chest, and in that very standing he knew what he had been asked about the affairs of this world and the next. Asked again, he answered: concerning ranks and expiations. Ranks are completing ablution in intense cold and awaiting prayer after prayer. He was told he spoke truly: whoever does so lives well, dies well, and becomes as sinless as the day his mother bore him. Expiations are feeding food, spreading peace, kind speech, and praying at night while people sleep. He then supplicated for righteous deeds, abandoning evil, love of the poor, forgiveness, repentance, and to be saved untested if trial is decreed. > **""You have spoken truly. O Allah, I ask You for righteous deeds, abandoning of evils, love of the poor, forgiveness, and that You turn to me. If You decree trial for a people, save me untested.""**"
"11745","Umm al-Tufayl, wife of Ubayy ibn Kab, may Allah be pleased with them, reported that she heard the Messenger of Allah, peace be upon him, say he saw his Lord in sleep in the form of a full-haired youth wearing leather socks, with two sandals of gold, and a covering of gold upon His face, and the narrator then related the full account. > **""I saw my Lord in sleep in the form of a full-haired youth wearing leather socks, with two sandals of gold and a covering of gold upon His face.""**"
"11746","Abd al-Rahman ibn Samurah, may Allah be pleased with him, reported that the Messenger of Allah, peace be upon him, came out and said he had seen a marvel last night. He saw a man of his community surrounded by angels, rescued by his ablution; a man under the punishment of the grave, rescued by his prayer; a man surrounded by devils, freed by remembrance of Allah; a man panting with thirst, given drink by the fast of Ramadan; a man in darkness on all sides, brought out by his pilgrimage and lesser pilgrimage; a man approached by the angel of death, defended by his upholding of kinship; a man shielding his face from the heat of Fire, shaded by his charity; a man seized by the angels of punishment, rescued by his enjoining of good and forbidding of evil; a man falling into Fire, lifted out by tears shed from fear of Allah; a man whose record fell to his left, taken to his right by his fear of Allah; a man whose scale was light, weighed down by his lending; a man trembling like a palm stalk, calmed by his good opinion of Allah; a man crawling, kneeling, and clinging on the bridge, raised and carried across by his blessings upon the Prophet; and a man reaching the gates of Paradise barred before him, taken by the hand by his testimony that none is worthy of worship but Allah and admitted into Paradise. > **""I saw last night a marvel. I saw a man of my community surrounded by angels, and his ablution came and rescued him.""**"
"11747","Jabir ibn Abdullah, may Allah be pleased with them both, reported that the Messenger of Allah, peace be upon him, said he saw as if a lump of dates was brought to him. He chewed one in his mouth, found a pit that harmed him and spat it out, then did the same with a second and a third. Abu Bakr said let him interpret it, and the Prophet told him to interpret. Abu Bakr said it is the army he dispatched: safe, gaining booty, meeting three successive men who invoke his covenant of protection and are spared. The Prophet said the angel spoke likewise. > **""Interpret it. The angel spoke likewise.""**"
"11748","Samurah ibn Jundub, may Allah be pleased with him, reported that a man said the Messenger of Allah, peace be upon him, said he saw as if a bucket was lowered from heaven. Abu Bakr came, seized its handles, and drank weakly, then Umar came and drank his fill, then Uthman came, seized it, drank, and it was snatched from him with some of it spilling upon him. > **""I saw as if a bucket was lowered from heaven. Abu Bakr drank weakly, Umar drank his fill, then Uthman drank and it was snatched from him.""**"
"11749","Jadah, may Allah be pleased with him, reported that the Prophet, peace be upon him, saw a dream concerning a man and sent for him. The man came and began narrating it to him. He was large-bellied, and the Prophet kept pointing with his finger at his belly, saying that had this been elsewhere it would have been better for him. > **""Had this been elsewhere, it would have been better for you.""**"
"11750","Anas, may Allah be pleased with him, reported that the Messenger of Allah, peace be upon him, said he saw in sleep as if the hilt-guard of his sword broke and as if he rode behind a ram. He interpreted the broken guard as the killing of a man of his people and the ram as killing the leader of the enemy. Thus Talhah ibn Abi Talhah, bearer of the idolaters' banner, was killed by the Messenger of Allah, and Hamzah ibn Abd al-Muttalib was killed. > **""I saw as if the hilt-guard of my sword broke and as if I rode behind a ram.""**"
"11751","Ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them both, reported that the Messenger of Allah, peace be upon him, took his sword Dhu al-Faqar as spoils at Badr, the same sword concerning which he saw a dream before Uhud. He said he saw as if there was a notch in Dhu al-Faqar, which he interpreted as killing among them; as if he rode behind a ram, which he interpreted as the ram of the enemy column; as if he was in strong armour, which he interpreted as Madinah; and cattle being slaughtered, which is goodness. > **""I saw as if there was a notch in my sword, as if I rode behind a ram, as if I was in strong armour, and cattle being slaughtered.""**"
"11752","Abu Said al-Khudri, may Allah be pleased with him, reported that he heard the Messenger of Allah, peace be upon him, say he had seen the Night of Decree, then was caused to forget it. Then he saw on his hands two bracelets of gold, disliked them, blew upon them, and they flew away. He interpreted them as the two liars, the one of Yemen and the one of Yamamah. > **""I saw the Night of Decree, then was caused to forget it. I saw two bracelets of gold, blew upon them, and they flew away.""**"
"11753","Ibn Umar, may Allah be pleased with them both, reported that the Messenger of Allah, peace be upon him, saw as if upon his forearms were two bracelets of gold. He blew upon them and they flew away, and said they are the two liars of his community, the one of Yemen and the one of Yamamah, who will not harm his community at all. > **""They are the two liars of my community, the one of Yemen and the one of Yamamah, and they will not harm my community at all.""**"
"11754","Abu Qatadah, may Allah be pleased with him, reported that the Messenger of Allah, peace be upon him, said that whoever sees him has seen the truth. > **""Whoever sees me has seen the truth.""**"
"11755","Abu Malik al-Ashjai reported from his father, may Allah be pleased with him, that the Messenger of Allah, peace be upon him, said that whoever sees him in sleep has truly seen him. > **""Whoever sees me in sleep has truly seen me.""**"
"11756","Abu Qatadah, may Allah be pleased with him, reported from the Messenger of Allah, peace be upon him, that the righteous dream is from Allah and the confused dream is from Satan. Whoever sees something he dislikes should spit to his left three times and seek refuge in Allah from Satan, for it will not harm him, and Satan cannot appear in his form. > **""The righteous dream is from Allah and the confused dream is from Satan. Whoever sees something he dislikes should spit to his left three times and seek refuge in Allah, for it will not harm him. Satan cannot appear in my form.""**"
"11757","Abu Said al-Khudri, may Allah be pleased with him, reported that the Messenger of Allah, peace be upon him, said that whoever sees him in sleep has truly seen him, for Satan cannot assume his form nor the form of the Kabah. > **""Whoever sees me in sleep has truly seen me, for Satan cannot assume my form nor the form of the Kabah.""**"
"11758","Abdullah ibn Amr, may Allah be pleased with them both, reported that the Messenger of Allah, peace be upon him, said that whoever sees him in sleep has seen him as if awake, and Satan cannot assume his form. In another wording, it is as if he saw him awake, and whoever sees him has seen truth. > **""Whoever sees me in sleep, it is as if he has seen me awake. Satan cannot assume my form.""**"
"11759","Abu Bakrah, may Allah be pleased with him, reported that the Prophet, peace be upon him, said that whoever sees him in sleep has seen him in wakefulness, and then mentioned the rest of the account. > **""Whoever sees me in sleep has seen me in wakefulness.""**"
"11760","Malik ibn Abdullah al-Khathami reported from the Messenger of Allah, peace be upon him, the like of the account of Abu Qatadah: that the Prophet, peace be upon him, said whoever sees him in sleep will see him in wakefulness, and Satan cannot assume his form. > **""Whoever sees me in sleep will see me in wakefulness, and Satan cannot assume my form.""**"
"11761","Abdullah, meaning Ibn Masud, may Allah be pleased with him, said that the Messenger of Allah, peace be upon him, could not be falsely imagined to one who saw him. > **""The Messenger of Allah cannot be falsely imagined to one who saw him.""**"
"11762","Khuzaymah ibn Thabit, may Allah be pleased with him, reported that he saw in sleep as if he prostrated upon the forehead of the Prophet, peace be upon him. He told the Messenger of Allah of that, who said that spirit meets spirit. The Prophet then inclined his head, and he placed his forehead upon the forehead of the Prophet. > **""Spirit meets spirit.""**"
"11763","The Prophet, peace be upon him, said to him to sit, prostrate, and do as he had seen in his dream. > **""Sit, prostrate, and do as you saw.""**"
"11764","Ibn Shihab reported from Umarah ibn Khuzaymah ibn Thabit al-Ansari, Khuzaymah being the one whose testimony the Messenger of Allah, peace be upon him, made equal to that of two men. Umarah reported from his uncle, a Companion of the Prophet, peace be upon him, that Khuzaymah ibn Thabit saw in sleep that he prostrated upon the forehead of the Messenger of Allah, peace be upon him. He came to the Messenger of Allah and mentioned it to him, so the Messenger of Allah lay down and he prostrated upon his forehead. > **""Khuzaymah saw that he prostrated upon the forehead of the Messenger of Allah, so the Messenger lay down and he prostrated upon his forehead.""**"
"11765","Khuzaymah ibn Thabit, may Allah be pleased with him, reported that he saw in his sleep that he kissed the Prophet, peace be upon him. He told him of it, so the Prophet stood for him and kissed his forehead. > **""Khuzaymah saw that he kissed the Prophet, so the Prophet stood and kissed his forehead.""**"
"11766","Al-Muthanna, meaning Ibn Said, reported that he heard Anas say that hardly a night passes over him but he sees in it his beloved, peace be upon him, and Anas said this with tears in his eyes. > **""Hardly a night passes over me but I see in it my beloved.""**"
"11767","Abu Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him, reported from the Prophet, peace be upon him, that a dream should not be narrated except to a scholar or a sincere adviser. > **""Do not narrate a dream except to a scholar or a sincere adviser.""**"
"11768","Abu al-Tufayl reported from the Prophet, peace be upon him, that he saw in sleep black sheep followed by dusty-white sheep, and interpreted the black sheep as the Arabs and the dusty ones as the non-Arabs. > **""I saw black sheep followed by dusty-white sheep. The black are the Arabs and the dusty are the non-Arabs.""**"
"11769","Abu Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him, reported from the Prophet, peace be upon him, that milk in a dream is the natural disposition. > **""Milk in a dream is the natural disposition.""**"
"11770","Anas, may Allah be pleased with him, reported that the Messenger of Allah, peace be upon him, used to interpret dreams by names. > **""The Messenger of Allah used to interpret dreams by names.""**"
"11771","Abu Bakrah, may Allah be pleased with him, reported that the Prophet, peace be upon him, said that whoever sees him in sleep has seen him in wakefulness. Whoever sees himself drinking milk, it is the natural disposition. Whoever sees himself wearing an iron coat, it is the strength of his religion. Whoever sees himself building a house, it is a deed he does. Whoever sees himself drowning, he is in the Fire. > **""Whoever sees me in sleep has seen me in wakefulness. Drinking milk is the natural disposition, an iron coat is the strength of religion, building a house is a deed one does, and drowning is in the Fire.""**"
"11772","Ibn Zumayl al-Juhani reported that when the Messenger of Allah, peace be upon him, had prayed dawn, he would say while folding his leg seventy times glorification and praise with seeking forgiveness, then say seventyfold becomes seven hundred, and no good is for one whose sins in a single day exceed seven hundred. He would then face the people, for he loved dreams and would ask whether any of them had seen anything. Ibn Zumayl said he answered that he had. The Prophet said he would meet good and be shielded from evil, good for them and evil upon their enemies, with praise belonging to the Lord of the worlds, and told him to narrate his dream. He said he saw all people upon a broad smooth-traveled path moving along, until the path overlooked a lush meadow of unmatched beauty, shimmering and dripping with dew, full of every pasture. The first party, upon reaching it, declared greatness, then mounted their rides along the path, some grazing and some gathering handfuls, and passed on. Then the multitude came, declared it an excellent dwelling, and inclined right and left. Seeing this, he kept to the path until its farthest end, where he saw the Messenger of Allah upon a pulpit of seven steps at its highest degree, with a dark coarse tall eloquent man on his right and a stocky reddish freckled man on his left whose speech they honored, and before them an elder most resembling the Prophet in build and face whom all sought, and before that a lean aged she-camel from which the Prophet seemed to shield himself. The Prophet's color changed for a moment, then cleared, and he explained: the smooth broad path is the guidance they bear; the meadow is the world and its flourishing life. He and his Companions passed without clinging to it. The second party after them, more numerous and weaker, took handfuls likewise. Then the multitude inclined into the meadow. As for him, he kept a sound path until meeting the Prophet. The pulpit of seven steps is the world of seven thousand years, with him in the last thousand. The dark man on the right is Musa, eminent through Allah's speech to him. The one on the left is Isa son of Maryam, honored for Allah's honoring him. The elder is their father Ibrahim, whom all follow. The she-camel is the Hour, rising upon them, with no prophet after him and no community after his community. After that the Messenger of Allah, peace be upon him, would not ask about a dream unless a man volunteered it. > **""You will meet good and be shielded from evil. Narrate your dream. The broad path is guidance, the meadow is the world, the pulpit of seven steps is seven thousand years with me in the last thousand, and the she-camel is the Hour. There is no prophet after me and no community after my community.""**"
"11773","Abdullah ibn Amr, may Allah be pleased with them both, reported that he saw in sleep as if upon one of his fingers was clarified butter and upon the other honey, and he licked them both. In the morning he mentioned it to the Prophet, peace be upon him, who said he reads the two Scriptures, the Torah and the Criterion, and thereafter he would read them both. > **""You read the two Scriptures, the Torah and the Criterion.""**"
"11774","Zakariyya ibn Ibrahim ibn Abdullah ibn Muti reported from his father from his grandfather that Muti ibn al-Aswad saw in his sleep that a bag of dates was gifted to him. He mentioned it to the Prophet, peace be upon him, who asked whether any of his young women was pregnant. He said yes, a woman of Banu Layth, mother of Abdullah. The Prophet said she would bear a boy. She bore a boy, who was brought to the Prophet, who named him Abdullah, performed tahnik with a date, and supplicated for blessing for him. > **""Is any of your young women pregnant? She will bear a boy.""**"
"11775","Abu Bakrah, may Allah be pleased with him, reported that the Prophet, peace be upon him, asked whether any of them had seen a dream. Aishah said she had seen three moons falling into her chamber. He told her that if her dream proved true, the best of the people of Paradise would be buried in her house. Then the Messenger of Allah, peace be upon him, passed away as the best of her moons, then Abu Bakr, then Umar, and they were buried in her house. > **""If your dream proves true, the best of the people of Paradise will be buried in your house.""**"
"11776","Ayyub reported from Nafi from Ibn Umar, or Muhammad ibn Sirin from Aishah, may Allah be pleased with her, that she said she saw as if three moons fell into her chamber. Abu Bakr said that if her dream proved true, the best of the people of the earth, three men, would be buried in her house. When the Prophet, peace be upon him, died, Abu Bakr said to her this was the best of her moons. Then Abu Bakr and Umar were buried in her house. > **""If your dream proves true, the best of the people of the earth will be buried in your house.""**"
